# CLData converter — `IExtensionGeomCLDataConverter`

## When to use

The toolpath is already calculated and you want to **change it on the way out**: lift every working
move by an offset, filter moves, apply a transformation, inject comments or custom CLData records,
collect statistics. You return a receiver that wraps the one ENCY gave you, and every CLD command flows
through your wrapper.

Not this kind if:
- you are producing the toolpath, not editing it → `type-operation-solver.mdc`;
- you want to post-process the final NC text → that is a postprocessor job, not an extension;
- you want a user-triggered one-off action → `type-utility.mdc`.

## Register it

The key is `geom_cldata_converter`, and the filter decides which operations you touch:

```json
{
  "name": "My CLData converter",
  "description": "What it changes",
  "module_path": "${extensionJsonFolder}/MyExtension.dll",
  "extensions": [
    {
      "geom_cldata_converter": {
        "id": "Extension.GeomCLDataConverter.MyExtension",
        "filterOperationTypes": {
          "filter_type": "exact",
          "operation_types": ["TST2DContouringOp"]
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

- `filter_type: "exact"` + `operation_types` — only those operation types (the shipping example uses
  exactly this snake_case spelling; the upstream reference calls the same fields `FilterType` /
  `OperationTypes`).
- `filter_type: "all"` — every operation in the project goes through your wrapper.
- `id` must match the branch in `CAMAPI.ExtensionFactory.Create`.

## Interface

```csharp
public interface IExtensionGeomCLDataConverter
{
    ICamApiCLDReceiver? GetCLDReceiverWrapper(ICamApiTechOperation operation,
                                              ICamApiCLDReceiver receiver,
                                              out TResultStatus ret);
    void FinalizeConverter();
}
```

You do **not** implement `ICamApiCLDReceiver` from scratch: the SDK ships
`CLDRecevierWrapperDefault` (yes, that is how the type is spelled) which delegates every command to the
wrapped receiver. Derive from it and override only the commands you change, calling `base.…` at the end.

## Skeleton

```csharp
using CAMAPI.DotnetHelper;
using CAMAPI.Extensions;
using CAMAPI.MCDFormerTypes;
using CAMAPI.ResultStatus;
using CAMAPI.TechOperation;
using STTypes;

namespace MyExtension;

public class MyCLDataConverter : IExtension, IExtensionGeomCLDataConverter
{
    public IExtensionInfo? Info { get; set; }

    private ICamApiCLDReceiver? _wrapper;

    public ICamApiCLDReceiver? GetCLDReceiverWrapper(ICamApiTechOperation operation,
                                                    ICamApiCLDReceiver receiver,
                                                    out TResultStatus ret)
    {
        ret = default;
        try
        {
            if (receiver == null)
                throw new Exception("Receiver is null");
            _wrapper = new MyWrapper(receiver);
            return _wrapper;
        }
        catch (Exception e)
        {
            ret.Code = TResultStatusCode.rsError;
            ret.Description = e.Message;
            return null;
        }
    }

    public void FinalizeConverter()
    {
        if (_wrapper is IDisposable disposable)
        {
            disposable.Dispose();
            _wrapper = null;
        }
    }
}

/// Lifts working moves; everything else passes through untouched.
public class MyWrapper : CLDRecevierWrapperDefault
{
    private int _currentFeed = (int)TFeedTypeFlag.affWorking;

    public MyWrapper(ICamApiCLDReceiver receiver) : base(receiver) { }

    public override void OutStandardFeed(int feed)
    {
        _currentFeed = feed;
        base.OutStandardFeed(feed);
    }

    public override void CutTo(TST3DPoint p)
    {
        if (_currentFeed == (int)TFeedTypeFlag.affWorking)
            p.Z += 20;
        base.CutTo(p);
    }
}
```

## Gotchas

- **Every override must call `base`** (or deliberately drop the command). A forgotten `base.CutTo(p)`
  silently deletes part of the toolpath — the operation still "calculates fine".
- **Track feed state yourself.** Whether a move is rapid, plunge or working is carried by the preceding
  `OutStandardFeed`/`OutFeed`/`OutPercentFeed` — override those to know what a `CutTo` means.
- **`receiver == null` is an error, not a silent `null` return.** Returning `null` without a status
  tells ENCY nothing.
- **`filter_type: "all"` puts your code in every operation of every project.** Filter narrowly unless
  you really mean it.
- **This is the hot path of calculation.** No dialogs, no per-command logging, no allocations you can
  avoid; a slow wrapper makes ENCY feel broken.
- **Release the wrapper in `FinalizeConverter`** — it holds a COM reference to the downstream receiver.

